@P0: is a divisor of the number of elements in the group. Since (Z/pZ)\nL27@P0: ∗ has p−1\nL28@P0: elements, we conclude that ordp(10) | p − 1. If ordp(10) = p − 1, we say\nL29@P0: that 10 is a primitive root mod p. The decimal expansion we have given for\nL30@P0: 1\nL31@P0: 7\nL32@P0: shows that 7 is a primitive root mod 10. Gauss’ table gives several other\nL33@P0: such examples and he must have asked himself whether there are infinitely\nL34@P0: many such primes, that is, primes p for which the decimal period is p − 1.\nL35@P0: Some light on this question can be shed on using the simple observation\nL36@P0: (due to Chebyshev) that if q = 4p + 1 is a prime with p a prime satisfying\nL37@P0: p ≡ 2(mod 5), then 10 is a primitive root modulo q. To this end notice\nL38@P0: that, a priori, ordq(10) = {1, 2, 4, p, 2p, 4p}. Now, using the law of quadratic\nL39@P0: reciprocity, see e.g. [27], one deduces that\nL40@P0: 102p ≡\nL41@P0: \u0012\nL42@P0: 10\nL43@P0: q\nL44@P0: \u0013\nL45@P0: =\nL46@P0: \u0012\nL47@P0: 2\nL48@P0: q\nL49@P0: \u0013 \u00125\nL50@P0: q\nL51@P0: \u0013\nL52@P0: = (−1) q\nL53@P0: 2−1\nL54@P0: 8\nL55@P0: \u0010q\nL56@P0: 5\nL57@P0: \u0011\nL58@P0: = −\nL59@P0: \u0012\nL60@P0: 4\nL61@P0: 5\nL62@P0: \u0013\nL63@P0: ≡ −1(mod q).\nL64@P0: Since no prime divisor of 104 − 1 satisfies the requirements (and hence\nL65@P0: ordq(10) ∤ 4), one sees that ordq(10) = 4p = q − 1.\nL66@P0: The logarithmic integral Li(x) is defined as R x\nL67@P0: 2\nL68@P0: dt/ log t. The Prime\nL69@P0: Number Theorem in the form π(x) := #{p ≤ x} ∼ Li(x) as x tends to\nL70@P0: infinity (i.e. the quotient of the r.h.s. and l.h.s. tends to 1 as x tends\nL71@P0: to infinity), suggests that the ‘probability that a number n is prime’ is\nL72@P0-1: 11/ log n. Thus for both n and 4n + 1 to be prime and n ≡ 2(mod 5) we\nL73@P1: expect a probability of 1/(5 log2 n), assuming independence of these three\nL74@P1: events. Since they are not, we have to correct by some positive constant\nL75@P1